Japanese sample EPUBs for exercising the reader by hand on a device. Kept apart
from test/epubs so this fork's own material, with its own provenance, does not
mix into the upstream set. Nothing here is loaded by the gtest suites; copy a
file to an SD card and open it.

Vertical writing (tategaki)
---------------------------

  test_vertical_ja.epub
  test_vertical_ja_minicss.epub

A Japanese book that declares page-progression-direction="rtl" with
dc:language ja, so the reader lays it out vertically. It walks through the
cases that vertical layout gets wrong most easily: ruby (145 instances,
including annotations long enough to overhang their base word), list items,
tilde variants (U+FF5E, U+301C, U+223C, U+02DC), fullwidth and halfwidth
digit runs of one, two and three digits, fullwidth operators (＝ ＜ ＞ ≠ ≒),
Greek letters, and line-break positions where kinsoku rules apply.

The two files carry byte-identical XHTML and differ only in stylesheet size:

  test_vertical_ja.epub          ~144 KB of CSS across 4 files
  test_vertical_ja_minicss.epub  ~2 KB of CSS in 1 file, holding only the
                                 rules the XHTML actually reaches

That pairing separates layout behaviour from CSS cost: the same text, the
same expected rendering, one book asking the parser to chew through seventy
times more stylesheet than the other.

Provenance
----------

Text by OSAKANA TARO, published on Kakuyomu under the handle memoro and
reused here by its author. CSS derived from the EBPAJ EPUB 3 production
guide template set, ver. 1.1.4. The EPUB itself is assembled by a private
PowerShell script, so these files are the artifact rather than the source.
